00:00:46.001 --> 00:00:46.919
A story:

13
00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:51.209
1,800 orders for 600 packets of onions.

14
00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:57.505
The festival sale's headline deal was 1 rupeesonions, 1 kg, limit one per customer.

15
00:00:57.785 --> 00:01:01.391
Each of the 50 busiest dark stores had about 12 packets.

16
00:01:01.671 --> 00:01:03.420
Inventory code looked like this:

17
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.306
Here is the Java code.

18
00:01:09.156 --> 00:01:16.388
The code had @Transactional, and the database was PostgreSQL at its default isolation level, READ COMMITTED.

19
00:01:16.668 --> 00:01:17.927
Every test passed.

20
00:01:18.207 --> 00:01:23.284
In production, 1,800 orders were confirmed for 600 packets.

21
00:01:23.564 --> 00:01:31.436
Pickers found empty shelves, and 1,200 customers received a sorry, item unavailable call after paying.

22
00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:33.615
Nothing was wrong with the database.

23
00:01:33.895 --> 00:01:41.447
At READ COMMITTED, two transactions can read the same available = 12, both compute 11, and both write 11.

24
00:01:41.727 --> 00:01:48.873
That's a lost update, and it's allowed at that isolation level. @Transactional doesn't make read-modify-write safe.

25
00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:51.287
The fix was one SQL statement:

26
00:01:52.637 --> 00:01:54.237
Here is the SQL code.

27
00:01:57.087 --> 00:01:57.769
