Part 2.

53
00:03:44.878 --> 00:03:46.777
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

54
00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:51.013
Intuition: the stock register at a kirana shop.

55
00:03:51.293 --> 00:03:53.555
A kirana shop owner keeps a ledger book:

56
00:03:53.835 --> 00:03:56.651
Tables are pages of the ledger with fixed columns.

57
00:03:56.931 --> 00:04:00.131
An index is the alphabetical tab strip on the side.

58
00:04:00.411 --> 00:04:02.288
Without it, you flip every page.

59
00:04:02.568 --> 00:04:05.491
A transaction is write all of this sale, or none of it.

60
00:04:05.771 --> 00:04:08.736
You never record the payment without reducing the stock.

61
00:04:09.016 --> 00:04:13.624
Isolation is what happens when two assistants update the same line at once.

62
00:04:13.904 --> 00:04:19.323
Does one wait, does one see the other's half-written line, or does one quietly overwrite the other?

63
00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:27.905
The WAL is the rough notebook the owner writes in first, so that if the ledger gets wet, the day can be reconstructed.

64
00:04:29.255 --> 00:04:30.556
ACID, precisely.

65
00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:32.201
Here is a quick table:

66
00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:38.199
Property, and What it guarantees, and What it does not guarantee, and Mechanism (PostgreSQL).

67
00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:39.481
Atomicity.

68
00:04:39.761 --> 00:04:40.956
What it guarantees:

69
00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:44.031
All statements in a transaction commit, or none do.

70
00:04:44.311 --> 00:04:45.804
What it does not guarantee:

71
00:04:46.084 --> 00:04:48.623
That concurrent transactions don't interfere.

72
00:04:48.903 --> 00:04:50.951
Mechanism (PostgreSQL):

73
00:04:51.231 --> 00:04:54.495
WAL + transaction status (commit log).

74
00:04:54.775 --> 00:04:55.841
Consistency.

75
00:04:56.121 --> 00:04:57.316
What it guarantees:

76
00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:01.991
Declared constraints hold after commit (PK, FK, UNIQUE, CHECK).

77
00:05:02.271 --> 00:05:03.764
What it does not guarantee:

78
00:05:04.044 --> 00:05:06.988
Business invariants you didn't declare (no overselling).

79
00:05:07.268 --> 00:05:09.316
Mechanism (PostgreSQL):

80
00:05:09.596 --> 00:05:10.684
Constraint checks.

81
00:05:10.964 --> 00:05:12.009
Isolation.

82
00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:13.484
What it guarantees:

83
00:05:13.764 --> 00:05:18.735
Concurrent transactions behave as if isolated to the degree of the chosen level.

84
00:05:19.015 --> 00:05:20.508
What it does not guarantee:

85
00:05:20.788 --> 00:05:23.156
Serializability, unless you choose it.

86
00:05:23.436 --> 00:05:25.484
Mechanism (PostgreSQL):

87
00:05:25.764 --> 00:05:29.156
MVCC snapshots + row locks + SSI.

88
00:05:29.436 --> 00:05:30.396
Durability.

89
00:05:30.676 --> 00:05:31.871
What it guarantees:

90
00:05:32.151 --> 00:05:33.985
Committed data survives a crash.

91
00:05:34.265 --> 00:05:35.759
What it does not guarantee:

92
00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:39.729
Survival of disk loss (that needs replication and backups).

93
00:05:40.009 --> 00:05:42.057
Mechanism (PostgreSQL):

94
00:05:42.337 --> 00:05:44.812
WAL flushed (fsync) at commit.

95
00:05:46.162 --> 00:05:46.802
Tip:

96
00:05:47.082 --> 00:05:49.663
Encode invariants in the database when you can.

97
00:05:49.943 --> 00:05:58.989
CHECK (available = 0) on the stock table turns an oversell bug into a failed transaction instead of a silent inconsistency.

98
00:05:59.269 --> 00:06:02.298
Constraints are the cheapest correctness tests you'll ever write.

99
00:06:03.648 --> 00:06:07.040
Storage internals: pages, heap, B+ trees, WAL.

100
00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.472
Pages and heap.

101
00:06:08.752 --> 00:06:13.979
PostgreSQL stores table rows in 8 KB pages in an unordered heap.

102
00:06:14.259 --> 00:06:23.112
Each row version (a tuple) has hidden header fields: xmin (the creating transaction) and xmax (the deleting or updating transaction).

103
00:06:23.392 --> 00:06:26.421
Those power MVCC (§2.7).

104
00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:28.195
B+ tree indexes.

105
00:06:28.475 --> 00:06:31.248
The default index is a balanced tree of pages.

106
00:06:31.528 --> 00:06:38.184
Leaf pages hold sorted keys with pointers (TIDs) to heap tuples, and are linked for range scans.

107
00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:40.878
Here is the diagram.

108
00:06:44.728 --> 00:06:46.093
Here is a quick table:

109
00:06:46.373 --> 00:06:48.549
Property, and Consequence for design.

110
00:06:48.829 --> 00:06:54.013
Height ~3 to 4 for millions to billions of rows (hundreds of keys per page):

111
00:06:54.293 --> 00:06:57.963
Point lookups cost ~3 to 4 page reads, mostly cached.

112
00:06:58.243 --> 00:06:59.352
Sorted leaves:

113
00:06:59.632 --> 00:07:02.896
Range queries and ORDER BY on the index prefix are cheap.

114
00:07:03.176 --> 00:07:04.648
Leftmost-prefix rule:

115
00:07:04.928 --> 00:07:11.157
An index on (customerid, createdat) helps WHERE customerid =? but not WHERE createdat? alone.

116
00:07:11.437 --> 00:07:14.445
Every index must be updated on insert or update:

117
00:07:14.725 --> 00:07:18.501
Indexes cost write throughput and storage (write amplification).

118
00:07:18.781 --> 00:07:22.280
Random-key inserts (UUIDv4) split pages everywhere:

119
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:29.600
Prefer time-ordered keys (UUIDv7, ULID, sequences) for large, insert-heavy tables (Ch 20).

120
00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:34.619
L1 (§5) shows the effect of a sorted structure:

121
00:07:34.899 --> 00:07:43.283
2 M rows and a customer or date-range query take a ~60 ms scan versus ~1 ms seek in plain Java.

122
00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:47.275
In a database the gap grows further once pages come from disk.

123
00:07:47.555 --> 00:07:49.561
Write-ahead log (WAL).

124
00:07:50.911 --> 00:07:53.577
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

125
00:07:53.857 --> 00:07:55.009
App to PG:

126
00:07:55.289 --> 00:07:57.060
UPDATE stock... then COMMIT.

127
00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:01.841
PG to SB: modify page in memory (page is now dirty).

128
00:08:02.121 --> 00:08:04.575
PG to WAL: append change record.

129
00:08:04.855 --> 00:08:08.844
PG to WAL: fsync at COMMIT (synchronouscommit = on).

130
00:08:09.124 --> 00:08:10.212
PG to App:

131
00:08:10.492 --> 00:08:11.921
COMMIT OK (durable).

132
00:08:15.771 --> 00:08:17.137
Here is a quick table:

133
00:08:17.417 --> 00:08:19.678
WAL fact, and Why you care.

134
00:08:19.958 --> 00:08:23.201
Commit latency about WAL fsync latency:

135
00:08:23.481 --> 00:08:27.001
Fast disks and group commit matter for write-heavy services.

136
00:08:27.281 --> 00:08:32.230
Replication ships WAL (physical) or decoded changes (logical):

137
00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:41.598
Replicas and CDC (Debezium, Ch 13) read the WAL. synchronouscommit = off trades durability for latency:

138
00:08:41.878 --> 00:08:45.249
Acceptable for clickstream, never for orders or payments.

139
00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:49.305
Full-page writes after checkpoints spike WAL volume:

140
00:08:49.585 --> 00:08:53.211
Tune checkpointtimeout and maxwalsize for write-heavy loads.

141
00:08:54.561 --> 00:08:55.948
Other storage engines:

142
00:08:56.228 --> 00:09:04.953
MySQL InnoDB uses a clustered index (the table is the primary-key B+ tree, and secondary indexes point to the PK).

143
00:09:05.233 --> 00:09:10.161
That makes PK range scans very fast and random PKs very expensive.

144
00:09:10.441 --> 00:09:16.991
LSM-tree engines (RocksDB, Cassandra) trade read cost for write throughput (Ch 06).

145
00:09:18.341 --> 00:09:22.458
Indexes: composite, covering, partial, and reading EXPLAIN, then.

146
00:09:22.738 --> 00:09:24.103
Here is a quick table:

147
00:09:24.383 --> 00:09:26.538
Index type, and Example, and Use when.

148
00:09:26.818 --> 00:09:27.885
Single column.

149
00:09:28.165 --> 00:09:29.039
Example:

150
00:09:29.319 --> 00:09:30.322
(customerid).

151
00:09:30.602 --> 00:09:31.477
Use when:

152
00:09:31.757 --> 00:09:33.165
One selective filter.

153
00:09:33.445 --> 00:09:34.341
Composite.

154
00:09:34.621 --> 00:09:35.495
Example:

155
00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:38.442
(customerid, createdat DESC, id DESC).

156
00:09:38.722 --> 00:09:39.597
Use when:

157
00:09:39.877 --> 00:09:43.461
Filter + sort (keyset pagination, Ch 04).

158
00:09:43.741 --> 00:09:44.935
Covering (INCLUDE).

159
00:09:45.215 --> 00:09:46.090
Example:

160
00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:48.589
(storeid, sku) INCLUDE (available).

161
00:09:48.869 --> 00:09:49.743
Use when:

162
00:09:50.023 --> 00:09:52.882
Answer from the index alone (index-only scan).

163
00:09:53.162 --> 00:09:53.994
Partial.

164
00:09:54.274 --> 00:09:55.149
Example:

165
00:09:55.429 --> 00:09:57.797
(expiresat) WHERE status = 'HELD'.

166
00:09:58.077 --> 00:09:58.951
Use when:

167
00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:03.093
You only ever query a small subset, such as active reservations.

168
00:10:03.373 --> 00:10:04.183
Unique.

169
00:10:04.463 --> 00:10:05.338
Example:

170
00:10:05.618 --> 00:10:07.111
UNIQUE (orderid, sku).

171
00:10:07.391 --> 00:10:08.266
Use when:

172
00:10:08.546 --> 00:10:10.231
An invariant, plus a lookup.

173
00:10:10.511 --> 00:10:11.514
Expression.

174
00:10:11.794 --> 00:10:12.669
Example:

175
00:10:12.949 --> 00:10:14.079
(lower(email)).

176
00:10:14.359 --> 00:10:15.234
Use when:

177
00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:17.114
Queries use the expression.

178
00:10:18.464 --> 00:10:19.147
GIN.

179
00:10:19.427 --> 00:10:20.301
Example:

180
00:10:20.581 --> 00:10:23.056
(tags) USING gin, full-text, JSONB.

181
00:10:23.336 --> 00:10:24.211
Use when:

182
00:10:24.491 --> 00:10:27.093
Containment queries, arrays, JSONB.

183
00:10:27.373 --> 00:10:28.099
BRIN.

184
00:10:28.379 --> 00:10:29.253
Example:

185
00:10:29.533 --> 00:10:31.197
(createdat) USING brin.

186
00:10:31.477 --> 00:10:32.352
Use when:

187
00:10:32.632 --> 00:10:36.643
Huge, naturally time-ordered append-only tables (tiny index).

188
00:10:37.993 --> 00:10:43.070
Composite column order: equality columns first, then range or sort columns:

189
00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.635
(storeid, status, createdat) for WHERE storeid =?

190
00:10:46.915 --> 00:10:48.174
AND status =?

191
00:10:48.454 --> 00:10:49.841
ORDER BY createdat.

192
00:10:50.121 --> 00:10:54.707
Put the most selective equality columns first when several queries share a prefix.

193
00:10:54.987 --> 00:11:00.086
Reading EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS), a representative plan (your numbers will differ):

194
00:11:01.436 --> 00:11:04.145
On screen: -- Before: no suitable index.

