00:11:49.929 --> 00:11:56.841
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idxorderscustomerkeyset ON orders (customerid. createdat DESC, id DESC);.

204
00:11:57.121 --> 00:12:15.873
Index Scan using idxorderscustomerkeyset on orders (cost=0.43..12.61 rows=11. width=96) (actual time=0.031..0.044 rows=9 loops=1).

205
00:12:16.153 --> 00:12:17.199
Index Cond:

206
00:12:17.479 --> 00:12:25.777
((customerid = 'cust-4242') AND (createdat = '2026 to 09 to 01')).

207
00:12:26.057 --> 00:12:27.572
The rest is in the chapter.

208
00:12:30.422 --> 00:12:31.787
Here is a quick table:

209
00:12:32.067 --> 00:12:34.883
Plan node or field, and Meaning, and Red flag.

210
00:12:35.163 --> 00:12:36.102
Seq Scan.

211
00:12:36.382 --> 00:12:37.171
Meaning:

212
00:12:37.451 --> 00:12:38.774
Reads the whole table.

213
00:12:39.054 --> 00:12:39.993
Red flag:

214
00:12:40.273 --> 00:12:42.321
On large tables in OLTP paths.

215
00:12:42.601 --> 00:12:44.649
Index Scan or Index Only Scan.

216
00:12:44.929 --> 00:12:45.718
Meaning:

217
00:12:45.998 --> 00:12:48.729
Uses the index (and heap unless index-only).

218
00:12:49.009 --> 00:12:49.947
Red flag:

219
00:12:50.227 --> 00:12:55.625
Index-only scans that still show heap fetches, then vacuum or visibility map lagging.

220
00:12:55.905 --> 00:12:57.206
Bitmap Heap Scan.

221
00:12:57.486 --> 00:12:58.275
Meaning:

222
00:12:58.555 --> 00:13:01.286
Collects many TIDs, then reads pages.

223
00:13:01.566 --> 00:13:02.505
Red flag:

224
00:13:02.785 --> 00:13:04.598
Fine for medium selectivity.

225
00:13:04.878 --> 00:13:07.523
Nested Loop or Hash Join or Merge Join.

226
00:13:07.803 --> 00:13:08.593
Meaning:

227
00:13:08.873 --> 00:13:10.089
Join strategies.

228
00:13:10.369 --> 00:13:11.307
Red flag:

229
00:13:11.587 --> 00:13:16.921
Nested loop with a large outer side. rows= estimate versus actual rows.

230
00:13:17.201 --> 00:13:17.990
Meaning:

231
00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.233
Planner estimate versus reality.

232
00:13:20.513 --> 00:13:21.451
Red flag:

233
00:13:21.731 --> 00:13:26.937
100 times differences mean stale statistics (ANALYZE) or correlated columns.

234
00:13:28.287 --> 00:13:29.951
Rows Removed by Filter.

235
00:13:30.231 --> 00:13:31.020
Meaning:

236
00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:32.900
Rows read and discarded.

237
00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.119
Red flag:

238
00:13:34.399 --> 00:13:37.001
Large numbers mean a missing or wrong index.

239
00:13:37.281 --> 00:13:38.711
Buffers: read=.

240
00:13:38.991 --> 00:13:39.780
Meaning:

241
00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:41.575
Pages read from disk.

242
00:13:41.855 --> 00:13:42.793
Red flag:

243
00:13:43.073 --> 00:13:47.169
High reads on hot paths mean a working set larger than RAM.

244
00:13:47.449 --> 00:13:49.284
Sort Method: external merge.

245
00:13:49.564 --> 00:13:50.353
Meaning:

246
00:13:50.633 --> 00:13:51.935
Sort spilled to disk.

247
00:13:52.215 --> 00:13:53.153
Red flag:

248
00:13:53.433 --> 00:13:56.932
Increase workmem for that query, or add an index for the order.

249
00:13:58.282 --> 00:13:59.029
Warning:

250
00:13:59.309 --> 00:14:00.482
Index hygiene.

251
00:14:00.762 --> 00:14:03.450
Every index slows writes and consumes memory.

252
00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:09.874
Review pgstatuserindexes for idxscan = 0 (unused) and for duplicate prefixes.

253
00:14:10.154 --> 00:14:17.109
In production, create indexes with CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY to avoid blocking writes (§2.12).

254
00:14:18.459 --> 00:14:21.296
Normalization versus denormalization.

255
00:14:21.576 --> 00:14:22.941
Here is a quick table:

256
00:14:23.221 --> 00:14:26.656
Normal form, and Rule of thumb, and Violation example.

257
00:14:26.936 --> 00:14:27.896
1NF.

258
00:14:28.176 --> 00:14:29.221
Rule of thumb:

259
00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:31.869
Atomic values, no repeating groups.

260
00:14:32.149 --> 00:14:37.696
Violation example: items = 'SKU1:2,SKU2:1' in one column.

261
00:14:37.976 --> 00:14:38.915
2NF.

262
00:14:39.195 --> 00:14:40.240
Rule of thumb:

263
00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:43.016
Non-key columns depend on the whole key.

264
00:14:43.296 --> 00:14:44.661
Violation example:

265
00:14:44.941 --> 00:14:50.061
In orderitem(orderid, sku, productname), productname depends only on sku.

266
00:14:50.341 --> 00:14:51.280
3NF.

267
00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:52.605
Rule of thumb:

268
00:14:52.885 --> 00:14:55.168
No dependency on non-key columns.

269
00:14:55.448 --> 00:14:59.075
Violation example: orders(customerid, customercity).

270
00:14:59.355 --> 00:15:00.336
BCNF.

271
00:15:00.616 --> 00:15:01.661
Rule of thumb:

272
00:15:01.941 --> 00:15:04.245
Every determinant is a candidate key.

273
00:15:04.525 --> 00:15:05.891
Violation example:

274
00:15:06.171 --> 00:15:07.323
Rare in practice.

275
00:15:08.673 --> 00:15:14.283
Normalize by default for write paths: one fact in one place means no update anomalies.

276
00:15:14.563 --> 00:15:18.638
Denormalize deliberately for reads, and keep it correct with a clear owner:

277
00:15:19.988 --> 00:15:21.353
Here is a quick table:

278
00:15:21.633 --> 00:15:30.444
Denormalization, and Why it's correct in the grocery platform. orderitem.productname, unitprice, gstrate copied at order time:

279
00:15:30.724 --> 00:15:39.855
Historical truth: the invoice must show the price and tax at purchase, not today's. orders.deliveryaddress snapshot (JSONB or columns):

280
00:15:40.135 --> 00:15:47.836
The customer may edit their address later, and the order shouldn't change. ordersummary read model in tracking (Ch 03):

281
00:15:48.116 --> 00:15:56.457
A different service, a different access pattern, fed by events (Ch 15). stock.available alongside onhand and reserved:

282
00:15:56.737 --> 00:16:00.791
A derived counter kept by the same transaction, protected by CHECK.

283
00:16:02.141 --> 00:16:02.887
Note:

284
00:16:03.167 --> 00:16:04.810
Snapshot versus reference.

285
00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.778
Ask should this value change when the source changes?

286
00:16:08.058 --> 00:16:11.407
If no (price at purchase, address at delivery), snapshot it.

287
00:16:11.687 --> 00:16:15.335
If yes (product description on the catalog page), reference it.

288
00:16:15.615 --> 00:16:20.053
Most denormalization bugs come from answering this question implicitly.

289
00:16:21.403 --> 00:16:23.515
Isolation levels and anomalies.

290
00:16:23.795 --> 00:16:25.160
Here is a quick table:

291
00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.320
Anomaly, and What happens, and Grocery example.

292
00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:29.560
Dirty read.

293
00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:30.779
What happens:

294
00:16:31.059 --> 00:16:32.509
Read uncommitted data.

295
00:16:32.789 --> 00:16:34.027
Grocery example:

296
00:16:34.307 --> 00:16:36.803
See a stock decrement that later rolls back.

297
00:16:37.083 --> 00:16:38.427
Non-repeatable read.

298
00:16:38.707 --> 00:16:39.645
What happens:

299
00:16:39.925 --> 00:16:42.635
The same row read twice gives different values.

300
00:16:42.915 --> 00:16:44.152
Grocery example:

301
00:16:44.432 --> 00:16:47.035
Price changes between validating and charging.

302
00:16:47.315 --> 00:16:48.189
Phantom.

303
00:16:48.469 --> 00:16:49.408
What happens:

304
00:16:49.688 --> 00:16:52.120
The same query returns different row sets.

305
00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.637
Grocery example:

306
00:16:53.917 --> 00:16:56.371
Slot booking count changes mid-transaction.

307
00:16:56.651 --> 00:16:57.717
Lost update.

308
00:16:57.997 --> 00:16:58.936
What happens:

309
00:16:59.216 --> 00:17:01.989
Two read-modify-writes, one overwrites the other.

310
00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:05.917
Grocery example: §1.1 onion oversell.

311
00:17:06.197 --> 00:17:07.136
Write skew.

312
00:17:07.416 --> 00:17:08.355
What happens:

313
00:17:08.635 --> 00:17:14.309
Two transactions read overlapping data, write different rows, and together break an invariant.

314
00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:15.827
Grocery example:

315
00:17:16.107 --> 00:17:21.824
Two riders each check ≥ 1 rider on shift at this store and both go off shift.

316
00:17:23.174 --> 00:17:29.851
What each level prevents in PostgreSQL (it differs from the SQL standard table and from MySQL):

317
00:17:31.201 --> 00:17:32.567
Here is a quick table:

318
00:17:32.847 --> 00:17:39.737
Level (PostgreSQL), and Dirty read, and Non-repeatable, and Phantom, and Lost update, and Write skew, and Notes.

319
00:17:40.017 --> 00:17:41.169
READ UNCOMMITTED.

320
00:17:41.449 --> 00:17:42.367
Dirty read:

321
00:17:42.647 --> 00:17:44.524
Prevented (treated as RC).

322
00:17:44.804 --> 00:17:46.276
READ COMMITTED (default).

323
00:17:46.556 --> 00:17:52.636
Lost update: for read-modify-write in the app; for SET x = x - 1 in SQL.

324
00:17:52.916 --> 00:17:53.727
Notes:

325
00:17:54.007 --> 00:17:55.585
New snapshot per statement.

326
00:17:55.865 --> 00:17:57.060
REPEATABLE READ.

327
00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:58.193
Phantom:

328
00:17:58.473 --> 00:17:59.412
(snapshot).

329
00:17:59.692 --> 00:18:00.759
Lost update:

330
00:18:01.039 --> 00:18:04.025
(errors with 40001 on concurrent update).

331
00:18:04.305 --> 00:18:05.116
Notes:

332
00:18:05.396 --> 00:18:06.783
Snapshot isolation.

333
00:18:08.133 --> 00:18:09.242
SERIALIZABLE.

334
00:18:09.522 --> 00:18:10.333
Notes:

335
00:18:10.613 --> 00:18:14.005
SSI: may abort with 40001, so retry.

336
00:18:15.355 --> 00:18:16.272
Watch out:

337
00:18:16.552 --> 00:18:18.899
The single most important line in this section.

338
00:18:19.179 --> 00:18:23.829
At READ COMMITTED, UPDATE stock SET available = available - 1 WHERE...

339
00:18:24.109 --> 00:18:26.072
AND available = 1 is safe.

340
00:18:26.352 --> 00:18:32.987
PostgreSQL re-evaluates the WHERE clause against the latest committed row after waiting for the row lock.

341
00:18:33.267 --> 00:18:38.856
The same logic written as SELECT, compute in Java, then UPDATE with a value is not safe.

342
00:18:39.136 --> 00:18:43.787
Push the arithmetic into the database, or lock, or use optimistic versioning.

343
00:18:45.137 --> 00:18:52.561
MySQL InnoDB differences: the default is REPEATABLE READ, and plain SELECTs read a consistent snapshot.

344
00:18:52.841 --> 00:18:53.673
SELECT...

345
00:18:53.953 --> 00:19:05.174
FOR UPDATE and UPDATEs read the latest data and take gap and next-key locks to prevent phantoms, which is a frequent source of unexpected lock waits and deadlocks on range predicates.

346
00:19:06.524 --> 00:19:09.425
MVCC: how readers don't block writers.

347
00:19:09.705 --> 00:19:16.532
With Multi-Version Concurrency Control, an UPDATE writes a new tuple version and marks the old one with xmax.

348
00:19:16.812 --> 00:19:20.417
Each transaction reads the versions visible to its snapshot.

349
00:19:21.767 --> 00:19:23.111
Here is the diagram.

350
00:19:26.961 --> 00:19:28.327
Here is a quick table:

351
00:19:28.607 --> 00:19:30.761
Consequence, and Design implication.

352
00:19:31.041 --> 00:19:34.625
Readers never block writers, and writers never block readers:

353
00:19:34.905 --> 00:19:37.103
Great concurrency for mixed workloads.

354
00:19:37.383 --> 00:19:40.711
Writers to the same row still serialize on the row lock:

355
00:19:40.991 --> 00:19:44.233
Hot rows (the 1 rupeesonion) are throughput-limited.

356
00:19:44.513 --> 00:19:46.135
Dead tuples accumulate:

357
00:19:46.415 --> 00:19:50.703
VACUUM or autovacuum must keep up, or tables and indexes bloat.

358
00:19:50.983 --> 00:19:54.204
Long-running transactions keep old snapshots alive:

359
00:19:54.484 --> 00:19:57.151
They block vacuum everywhere, and bloat grows.

360
00:19:57.431 --> 00:20:01.591
Kill idle-in-transaction sessions (idleintransactionsessiontimeout).

361
00:20:02.941 --> 00:20:10.557
HOT updates (heap-only tuples) avoid index updates when no indexed column changes and the page has room:

362
00:20:10.837 --> 00:20:18.325
Keep frequently updated columns (available) out of indexes when possible, and use fillfactor 100 for hot tables.

363
00:20:18.605 --> 00:20:20.418
Transaction ID wraparound:

364
00:20:20.698 --> 00:20:22.597
Vacuum must freeze old tuples.

365
00:20:22.877 --> 00:20:25.074
Monitor age(datfrozenxid).

366
00:20:26.424 --> 00:20:29.133
Locking: optimistic versus pessimistic.

367
00:20:29.413 --> 00:20:30.779
Here is a quick table:

368
00:20:31.059 --> 00:20:33.747
Strategy, and How, and Best when, and Cost.

369
00:20:34.027 --> 00:20:35.755
Atomic conditional update.

370
00:20:36.035 --> 00:20:36.696
How:

371
00:20:36.976 --> 00:20:37.829
UPDATE...

372
00:20:38.109 --> 00:20:39.880
SET x = x - n WHERE...

373
00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:41.461
AND x = n.

374
00:20:41.741 --> 00:20:42.637
Best when:

375
00:20:42.917 --> 00:20:45.392
Counters and simple invariants on one row.

376
00:20:45.672 --> 00:20:46.397
Cost:

377
00:20:46.677 --> 00:20:49.493
One statement, and the row lock is held briefly.

378
00:20:49.773 --> 00:20:50.883
Default choice.

379
00:20:51.163 --> 00:20:52.613
Optimistic (version).

380
00:20:52.893 --> 00:20:53.555
How:

381
00:20:53.835 --> 00:20:54.688
UPDATE...

382
00:20:54.968 --> 00:20:56.184
WHERE id =?

383
00:20:56.464 --> 00:21:01.307
AND version =? where 0 rows means a conflict (JPA @Version).

384
00:21:01.587 --> 00:21:02.483
Best when:

385
00:21:02.763 --> 00:21:07.008
Low contention, user-facing edits (Ch 04 ETags).

386
00:21:07.288 --> 00:21:08.013
Cost:

387
00:21:08.293 --> 00:21:10.811
Retries under contention; wasted work.

388
00:21:11.091 --> 00:21:12.093
Pessimistic.

389
00:21:12.373 --> 00:21:13.035
How:

390
00:21:13.315 --> 00:21:14.147
SELECT...

391
00:21:14.427 --> 00:21:15.899
FOR UPDATE then update.

392
00:21:16.179 --> 00:21:17.075
Best when:

393
00:21:17.355 --> 00:21:21.173
Multi-row read-then-decide logic that can't be one statement.

394
00:21:21.453 --> 00:21:22.179
Cost:

395
00:21:22.459 --> 00:21:25.957
Holds locks for the transaction duration; deadlock risk.

396
00:21:27.307 --> 00:21:28.715
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED.

397
00:21:28.995 --> 00:21:29.657
How:

398
00:21:29.937 --> 00:21:31.643
Take the next unlocked rows.

399
00:21:31.923 --> 00:21:32.819
Best when:

400
00:21:33.099 --> 00:21:35.894
Work queues (outbox relay, webhook worker).

401
00:21:36.174 --> 00:21:36.899
Cost:

402
00:21:37.179 --> 00:21:39.718
Not for correctness checks on specific rows.

403
00:21:39.998 --> 00:21:41.193
FOR UPDATE NOWAIT.

404
00:21:41.473 --> 00:21:42.134
How:

405
00:21:42.414 --> 00:21:44.078
Fail immediately if locked.

406
00:21:44.358 --> 00:21:45.254
Best when:

407
00:21:45.534 --> 00:21:47.241
UI someone else is editing.

408
00:21:47.521 --> 00:21:48.246
Cost:

409
00:21:48.526 --> 00:21:49.571
Error handling.

410
00:21:49.851 --> 00:21:51.067
Advisory locks.

411
00:21:51.347 --> 00:21:53.502
How: pgadvisoryxactlock(key).

412
00:21:53.782 --> 00:21:54.678
Best when:

413
00:21:54.958 --> 00:21:58.414
Serializing a logical operation that isn't a row.

414
00:21:58.694 --> 00:21:59.419
Cost:

415
00:21:59.699 --> 00:22:01.683
Easy to misuse; must be scoped.

416
00:22:01.963 --> 00:22:03.073
SERIALIZABLE.

417
00:22:03.353 --> 00:22:04.014
How:

418
00:22:04.294 --> 00:22:06.235
Let SSI detect conflicts.

419
00:22:06.515 --> 00:22:07.411
Best when:

420
00:22:07.691 --> 00:22:10.422
Complex invariants across rows (write skew).

421
00:22:10.702 --> 00:22:11.427
Cost:

422
00:22:11.707 --> 00:22:14.545
Aborts under contention; needs retry loops.

423
00:22:15.895 --> 00:22:16.791
Deadlocks.

424
00:22:17.071 --> 00:22:22.724
Transaction A locks row 1 and waits for row 2, while B locks row 2 and waits for row 1.

425
00:22:23.004 --> 00:22:28.764
PostgreSQL detects this after deadlocktimeout (1 s by default) and aborts one of them.

426
00:22:29.044 --> 00:22:31.924
Prevention: lock rows in a consistent order.

427
00:22:32.204 --> 00:22:36.343
For a multi-SKU order, sort lines by sku before updating.

428
00:22:36.623 --> 00:22:42.276
Mitigation: keep transactions short, and retry on SQLSTATE 40P01.

429
00:22:43.626 --> 00:22:45.781
JPA or Hibernate pitfalls.

430
00:22:46.061 --> 00:22:47.426
Here is a quick table:

431
00:22:47.706 --> 00:22:49.519
Pitfall, and Symptom, and Fix.

432
00:22:49.799 --> 00:22:51.143
N+1 selects.

433
00:22:51.423 --> 00:22:52.234
Symptom:

434
00:22:52.514 --> 00:22:59.426
Loading 20 orders fires 1 + 20 item queries (+ 20 more for each lazy association touched).

435
00:22:59.706 --> 00:23:00.431
Fix:

436
00:23:00.711 --> 00:23:11.271
JOIN FETCH / @EntityGraph for single queries; @BatchSize or hibernate.defaultbatchfetchsize for collections; DTO projections for read APIs.

437
00:23:11.551 --> 00:23:13.535
Collection fetch + pagination.

438
00:23:13.815 --> 00:23:14.626
Symptom:

439
00:23:14.906 --> 00:23:21.967
Hibernate warns it's paginating in memory (HHH90003004) and loads everything.

440
00:23:22.247 --> 00:23:22.973
Fix:

441
00:23:23.253 --> 00:23:28.245
Two queries: page the parent ids (keyset), then fetch children WHERE orderid IN (...).

442
00:23:28.525 --> 00:23:31.789
Open Session in View (on by default in Spring Boot).

443
00:23:32.069 --> 00:23:32.879
Symptom:

444
00:23:33.159 --> 00:23:40.541
Lazy loads fire during JSON serialization, outside your transaction, holding a connection for the whole request.

445
00:23:40.821 --> 00:23:46.389
Fix: spring.jpa.open-in-view=false; fetch what you need in the service layer.

446
00:23:47.739 --> 00:23:49.765
LazyInitializationException.

447
00:23:50.045 --> 00:23:50.856
Symptom:

448
00:23:51.136 --> 00:23:55.019
Accessing a lazy association after the transaction closed.

449
00:23:55.299 --> 00:23:56.024
Fix:

450
00:23:56.304 --> 00:24:00.379
Fetch explicitly in the query; don't return entities from services.

451
00:24:00.659 --> 00:24:03.411
IDENTITY ids disable insert batching.

452
00:24:03.691 --> 00:24:04.501
Symptom:

453
00:24:04.781 --> 00:24:08.003
1,000 inserts = 1,000 round trips.

454
00:24:08.283 --> 00:24:09.008
Fix:

455
00:24:09.288 --> 00:24:17.693
Use SEQUENCE with a pooled optimizer (allocationSize = 50), plus hibernate.jdbc.batchsize and orderinserts.

456
00:24:17.973 --> 00:24:19.829
Dirty checking on large sessions.

457
00:24:20.109 --> 00:24:20.920
Symptom:

458
00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:23.141
Slow flushes, high CPU.

459
00:24:23.421 --> 00:24:29.800
Fix: @Transactional(readOnly = true) for reads; projections instead of managed entities.

460
00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:32.085
Read-modify-write via entities.

461
00:24:32.365 --> 00:24:33.176
Symptom:

462
00:24:33.456 --> 00:24:35.824
Lost updates (§1.1).

463
00:24:36.104 --> 00:24:42.717
Fix: @Version, or @Modifying @Query(update... set x = x -:n where... and x =:n).

464
00:24:44.067 --> 00:24:47.374
@Transactional on private methods or self-invocation.

465
00:24:47.654 --> 00:24:48.465
Symptom:

466
00:24:48.745 --> 00:24:50.366
Silently no transaction.

467
00:24:50.646 --> 00:24:51.371
Fix:

468
00:24:51.651 --> 00:24:55.641
Call through the Spring proxy; transactions on public service methods.

469
00:24:55.921 --> 00:24:58.182
Long transactions around remote calls.

470
00:24:58.462 --> 00:24:59.273
Symptom:

471
00:24:59.553 --> 00:25:02.411
Connections held for seconds; pool exhaustion.

472
00:25:02.691 --> 00:25:03.417
Fix:

473
00:25:03.697 --> 00:25:08.731
Do remote calls outside the transaction; keep transactions to DB work only.

474
00:25:09.011 --> 00:25:12.211
CascadeType.ALL + orphanRemoval everywhere.

475
00:25:12.491 --> 00:25:13.302
Symptom:

476
00:25:13.582 --> 00:25:15.950
Unexpected deletes, huge cascades.

477
00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:16.955
Fix:

478
00:25:17.235 --> 00:25:20.307
Cascade only within an aggregate (Ch 33).

479
00:25:20.587 --> 00:25:24.022
Entities in equals or hashCode on generated ids.

480
00:25:24.302 --> 00:25:25.113
Symptom:

481
00:25:25.393 --> 00:25:27.142
Broken sets before persist.

482
00:25:27.422 --> 00:25:28.147
Fix:

483
00:25:28.427 --> 00:25:31.926
Use a business key or id-based equality done carefully.

484
00:25:33.276 --> 00:25:44.113
Detecting N+1 early: enable Hibernate statistics or a datasource proxy in tests, and assert the query count for key endpoints (L2 or L3 below).

485
00:25:45.463 --> 00:25:48.237
Connection pooling: sizing HikariCP.

486
00:25:48.517 --> 00:25:50.287
Two constraints must both hold:

487
00:25:50.567 --> 00:25:57.885
App side (Little's Law, Ch 02): busy connections = request rate times DB time per request.

488
00:25:58.165 --> 00:26:04.117
At 850 checkouts per second times 20 ms that's ~17 across the fleet.

489
00:26:04.397 --> 00:26:05.613
Database side:

490
00:26:05.893 --> 00:26:12.741
PostgreSQL does best with a small number of active connections, roughly a small multiple of CPU cores.

491
00:26:13.021 --> 00:26:20.829
HikariCP's guidance starts from connections about (cores times 2) + effectivespindlecount for the whole database.

492
00:26:21.109 --> 00:26:26.741
Beyond that, adding connections adds contention (context switching, lock waits), not throughput.

493
00:26:28.091 --> 00:26:29.456
Here is a quick table:

494
00:26:29.736 --> 00:26:32.424
Setting, and Guidance. maximum-pool-size:

495
00:26:32.704 --> 00:26:41.856
From Little's Law per pod times headroom, and Σ pods times pool at most DB budget (Ch 02 connection budget). minimum-idle:

496
00:26:42.136 --> 00:26:48.600
Equal to max (fixed-size pool) for predictable latency, unless many pods idle. connection-timeout:

497
00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:55.216
250 ms, 1 s: fail fast instead of queuing behind a sick DB. max-lifetime:

498
00:26:55.496 --> 00:27:02.984
Below any network or DB idle kill (for example 25 to 30 min), with jitter built in. idle-timeout:

499
00:27:03.264 --> 00:27:07.232
Only relevant when minimum-idle max. leak-detection-threshold:

500
00:27:07.512 --> 00:27:12.184
2 to 5 s in staging to catch connections held across remote calls.

501
00:27:12.464 --> 00:27:13.637
Statement timeout:

502
00:27:13.917 --> 00:27:19.763
SET statementtimeout per role or query: no query should run unbounded on an OLTP path.

503
00:27:21.113 --> 00:27:23.395
PgBouncer (transaction mode):

504
00:27:23.675 --> 00:27:27.793
When Σ pods times pool exceeds what PostgreSQL should hold.

505
00:27:28.073 --> 00:27:35.134
Breaks session features (session-level prepared statements in older setups, SET, advisory session locks, LISTEN).

506
00:27:36.484 --> 00:27:37.124
Tip:

507
00:27:37.404 --> 00:27:40.839
Virtual threads make pool limits more important, not less.

508
00:27:41.119 --> 00:27:44.916
With virtual threads, thousands of requests can run concurrently.

509
00:27:45.196 --> 00:27:49.015
The connection pool becomes the concurrency limiter for the database.

510
00:27:49.295 --> 00:27:54.159
That's good, as long as the pool is sized deliberately and connection-timeout is short.

511
00:27:55.509 --> 00:27:57.813
Read replicas and replication lag.

512
00:27:58.093 --> 00:28:08.589
Streaming replicas apply the primary's WAL asynchronously (typically milliseconds, sometimes seconds under load, or much longer during long queries on the replica).

513
00:28:09.939 --> 00:28:11.304
Here is a quick table:

514
00:28:11.584 --> 00:28:13.269
Read type, and Where, and Why.

515
00:28:13.549 --> 00:28:16.621
Right after the user's own write (my order just placed).

516
00:28:16.901 --> 00:28:17.563
Where:

517
00:28:17.843 --> 00:28:21.192
Primary, or a replica that has caught up to the write's LSN.

518
00:28:21.472 --> 00:28:22.155
Why:

519
00:28:22.435 --> 00:28:23.501
Read-your-writes.

520
00:28:23.781 --> 00:28:26.768
Decision reads inside a transaction (stock check).

521
00:28:27.048 --> 00:28:27.709
Where:

522
00:28:27.989 --> 00:28:28.843
Primary.

523
00:28:29.123 --> 00:28:29.805
Why:

524
00:28:30.085 --> 00:28:31.195
Must be current.

525
00:28:31.475 --> 00:28:35.656
Browsing catalog, reports, order history older than a few seconds.

526
00:28:35.936 --> 00:28:36.597
Where:

527
00:28:36.877 --> 00:28:37.752
Replica.

528
00:28:38.032 --> 00:28:38.715
Why:

529
00:28:38.995 --> 00:28:40.275
Offload the primary.

530
00:28:40.555 --> 00:28:42.027
Analytics and exports.

531
00:28:42.307 --> 00:28:42.968
Where:

532
00:28:43.248 --> 00:28:46.939
A separate analytics replica or warehouse (Ch 21).

533
00:28:47.219 --> 00:28:47.901
Why:

534
00:28:48.181 --> 00:28:53.045
Long queries on OLTP replicas cause replay lag or query cancellation.

535
00:28:54.395 --> 00:29:11.846
Read-your-writes techniques: route by @Transactional(readOnly) plus sticky to primary for N seconds after a write (session or cookie); or compare the write's LSN (pgcurrentwallsn()) with the replica's pglastwalreplaylsn() (precise, implemented in L4).

536
00:29:13.196 --> 00:29:15.095
Schema changes without downtime.

537
00:29:15.375 --> 00:29:16.740
