00:35:02.129 --> 00:35:11.899
ADR-024 Atomic conditional updates for counters; ADR-025 Replica routing with LSN-based read-your-writes.

674
00:35:13.249 --> 00:35:13.889
1.

675
00:35:14.169 --> 00:35:15.577
Context and Problem.

676
00:35:15.857 --> 00:35:22.236
A read-modify-write in JPA oversold flash-sale items 3 times (§1.1).

677
00:35:22.516 --> 00:35:30.004
Deadlocks appeared on multi-item orders during peaks, and order-history queries loaded items with N+1 selects.

678
00:35:30.284 --> 00:35:30.988
2.

679
00:35:31.268 --> 00:35:32.612
Goals or Non-Goals.

680
00:35:32.892 --> 00:35:46.865
Goals: never oversell; reserve all-or-nothing; no deadlocks by construction; p99 reservation at most 50 ms at P90 load; history reads offloaded to replicas without breaking read-your-writes.

681
00:35:47.145 --> 00:35:57.620
Non-goals: a national single-SKU flash deal above hot-row limits (Ch 46 bucketing or queueing); multi-region writes (Ch 27).

682
00:35:58.970 --> 00:35:59.717
3.

683
00:35:59.997 --> 00:36:00.957
Requirements.

684
00:36:02.307 --> 00:36:03.672
Here is a quick table:

685
00:36:03.952 --> 00:36:05.424
ID, and Requirement.

686
00:36:05.704 --> 00:36:06.643
FR-1:

687
00:36:06.923 --> 00:36:09.888
Reserve all lines of an order at one store, or none.

688
00:36:10.168 --> 00:36:11.107
FR-2:

689
00:36:11.387 --> 00:36:14.117
Confirm, release and expire reservations.

690
00:36:14.397 --> 00:36:15.443
NFR-1:

691
00:36:15.723 --> 00:36:22.187
Invariant: available = 0 and available = onhand - reserved, enforced in the DB.

692
00:36:22.467 --> 00:36:23.512
NFR-2:

693
00:36:23.792 --> 00:36:32.368
Reservation p99 at most 50 ms; retry on 40P01 or 40001 at most 3 times.

694
00:36:32.648 --> 00:36:33.715
NFR-3:

695
00:36:33.995 --> 00:36:37.621
Reserving the same order twice is idempotent (orderid UNIQUE).

696
00:36:37.901 --> 00:36:38.989
NFR-4:

697
00:36:39.269 --> 00:36:43.003
Expired holds return stock within 60 s of expiresat.

698
00:36:43.283 --> 00:36:44.328
NFR-5:

699
00:36:44.608 --> 00:36:49.216
Order or stock views after a user's write reflect that write (read-your-writes).

700
00:36:50.566 --> 00:36:51.270
4.

701
00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:52.830
Capacity estimates.

702
00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:55.545
Here is a quick table:

703
00:36:55.825 --> 00:36:57.148
Item, and Estimate.

704
00:36:57.428 --> 00:36:58.495
Stock rows:

705
00:36:58.775 --> 00:37:10.743
500 stores times ~6,000 SKUs = 3 M rows, ~150 B each about 450 MB + indexes, so it fits in RAM.

706
00:37:11.023 --> 00:37:19.812
Reservations at P90 peak: ~420 orders per second times 1.2 attempts about 500 per second.

707
00:37:20.092 --> 00:37:21.415
Row updates at peak:

708
00:37:21.695 --> 00:37:36.607
500 per second times ~8 lines about 4 K stock UPDATEs per second + 4 K line inserts + 500 reservation inserts + 500 outbox inserts about ~9 K writes per second.

709
00:37:36.887 --> 00:37:48.087
Hottest row: ~50 reservations per second on one store-SKU (Ch 02) versus ~500 to 1,000 per second row-lock capacity:

710
00:37:48.367 --> 00:37:49.135
OK.

711
00:37:49.415 --> 00:37:54.151
Transaction duration: ~8 short statements about 5 to 10 ms.

712
00:37:54.431 --> 00:37:55.583
Busy connections:

713
00:37:55.863 --> 00:38:00.983
500 tx per second times 10 ms about 5 (Little's Law); pools:

714
00:38:01.263 --> 00:38:04.676
6 pods times 8 = 48 at most budget.

715
00:38:06.026 --> 00:38:15.413
WAL volume: ~9 K writes per second times ~200 B about 1.8 MB per second (+ full-page writes).

716
00:38:16.763 --> 00:38:17.680
Success:

717
00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:19.432
What the estimate tells us.

718
00:38:19.712 --> 00:38:23.083
One PostgreSQL primary handles this comfortably.

719
00:38:23.363 --> 00:38:31.085
The dataset fits in memory, and ~9 K writes per second is well within a tuned 8 to 16 vCPU instance.

720
00:38:31.365 --> 00:38:36.293
The risks are correctness (lost updates, deadlocks) and hot rows, not raw capacity.

721
00:38:37.643 --> 00:38:39.073
