00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:09.626
ADR-025:

949
00:53:09.906 --> 00:53:12.658
Replica routing with LSN-based read-your-writes.

950
00:53:12.938 --> 00:53:13.855
Context.

951
00:53:14.135 --> 00:53:24.973
Read traffic on inventory and orders (history, store dashboards) is ~10 times write traffic and competes with reservations for primary CPU during peaks.

952
00:53:25.253 --> 00:53:30.330
Naive replica reads show users stale data immediately after their own writes.

953
00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:32.555
Decision.

954
00:53:32.835 --> 00:53:39.917
(1) @Transactional(readOnly = true) routes to a replica via LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy.

955
00:53:40.197 --> 00:53:45.829
(2) Writes return a consistency token (the primary's WAL LSN after commit).

956
00:53:46.109 --> 00:53:57.459
The BFF echoes it on subsequent reads for that user for ~30 s, and the service reads from the replica only if it has replayed past that LSN, otherwise from the primary.

957
00:53:57.739 --> 00:54:02.389
(3) Decision reads inside write transactions always use the primary.

958
00:54:02.669 --> 00:54:05.933
(4) Replicas used by OLTP never serve analytics.

959
00:54:06.213 --> 00:54:09.072
Those go to a dedicated replica or warehouse.

960
00:54:10.422 --> 00:54:11.446
Consequences.

961
00:54:11.726 --> 00:54:15.630
(+) ~70 to 80 % of reads leave the primary.

962
00:54:15.910 --> 00:54:18.683
(+) Users never see their own write disappear.

963
00:54:18.963 --> 00:54:22.227
(minus ) An extra cheap query per token-bearing read.

964
00:54:22.507 --> 00:54:26.347
(minus ) The token has to be plumbed through BFF responses.

965
00:54:26.627 --> 00:54:31.854
(minus ) During large replica lag, token-bearing reads concentrate on the primary.

966
00:54:32.134 --> 00:54:33.905
Alert on lag above 1 s.

967
00:54:34.185 --> 00:54:45.939
Revisit when: write volume outgrows one primary (functional split or sharding, Ch 11), or multi-region reads need a different consistency model (Ch 27).

