01:05:41.921 --> 01:05:43.627
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1224
01:05:44.877 --> 01:05:45.517
1.

1225
01:05:45.797 --> 01:05:48.165
Read-modify-write in application code.

1226
01:05:49.515 --> 01:05:59.094
Here is the Java code. before: two transactions read 12, both write 11. after: the database does the arithmetic and the check atomically.

1227
01:06:01.944 --> 01:06:02.648
2.

1228
01:06:02.928 --> 01:06:05.552
Believing @Transactional means serialized.

1229
01:06:05.832 --> 01:06:06.643
Symptom:

1230
01:06:06.923 --> 01:06:09.141
It's in a transaction, so it can't race.

1231
01:06:09.421 --> 01:06:10.147
Fix:

1232
01:06:10.427 --> 01:06:11.941
Know your isolation level.

1233
01:06:12.221 --> 01:06:17.277
READ COMMITTED allows lost updates in app-level read-modify-write and write skew.

1234
01:06:17.557 --> 01:06:18.304
3.

1235
01:06:18.584 --> 01:06:20.653
Remote calls inside transactions.

1236
01:06:22.003 --> 01:06:36.873
Here is the Java code. before: holds a DB connection + row locks while waiting on Razorpay (seconds). after: short DB transactions; remote call outside; outbox or saga for coordination (Ch 13).

1237
01:06:39.723 --> 01:06:40.427
4.

1238
01:06:40.707 --> 01:06:43.331
N+1 queries hidden by lazy loading.

1239
01:06:43.611 --> 01:06:44.421
Symptom:

1240
01:06:44.701 --> 01:06:49.096
The endpoint does 200 queries, and nobody notices until the peak.

1241
01:06:49.376 --> 01:06:50.101
Fix:

1242
01:06:50.381 --> 01:06:55.139
Batch or fetch explicitly, disable open-in-view, and use query-count tests.

1243
01:06:55.419 --> 01:06:56.187
5.

1244
01:06:56.467 --> 01:06:58.259
Indexing everything (or nothing).

1245
01:06:58.539 --> 01:06:59.349
Symptom:

1246
01:06:59.629 --> 01:07:03.704
Slow writes and bloated storage, or sequential scans on hot paths.

1247
01:07:03.984 --> 01:07:04.709
Fix:

1248
01:07:04.989 --> 01:07:11.539
Design indexes from access patterns, verify with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS), and drop unused ones.

1249
01:07:11.819 --> 01:07:12.565
6.

1250
01:07:12.845 --> 01:07:15.171
Offset pagination on large tables.

1251
01:07:15.451 --> 01:07:16.965
See Ch 04.

1252
01:07:17.245 --> 01:07:18.248
Use keyset.

1253
01:07:19.598 --> 01:07:20.345
7.

1254
01:07:20.625 --> 01:07:22.758
Unbounded queries and no timeouts.

1255
01:07:23.038 --> 01:07:23.849
Symptom:

1256
01:07:24.129 --> 01:07:27.862
One report query holds locks and connections for 10 minutes.

1257
01:07:28.142 --> 01:07:34.627
Fix: statementtimeout per role, LIMIT on every OLTP query, and analytics on replicas or a warehouse.

1258
01:07:34.907 --> 01:07:35.590
8.

1259
01:07:35.870 --> 01:07:38.217
Blocking schema changes in a deploy.

1260
01:07:39.567 --> 01:07:45.476
Here is the SQL code. before: blocks writes on a 3M-row hot table for the whole build.

1261
01:07:48.326 --> 01:07:49.073
9.

1262
01:07:49.353 --> 01:07:51.273
Pool size = big number.

1263
01:07:51.553 --> 01:07:52.363
Symptom:

1264
01:07:52.643 --> 01:08:00.174
50 times 30 pods = 1,500 connections, and the database spends its time context-switching.

1265
01:08:00.454 --> 01:08:01.179
Fix:

1266
01:08:01.459 --> 01:08:05.683
Size from Little's Law and the DB core count, and add PgBouncer.

1267
01:08:05.963 --> 01:08:06.667
10.

1268
01:08:06.947 --> 01:08:12.814
Random UUIDv4 primary keys on huge insert-heavy tables (especially InnoDB).

1269
01:08:13.094 --> 01:08:13.905
Symptom:

1270
01:08:14.185 --> 01:08:17.257
Page splits, index bloat, and poor cache locality.

1271
01:08:17.537 --> 01:08:18.262
Fix:

1272
01:08:18.542 --> 01:08:22.979
Time-ordered ids (UUIDv7, ULID, sequences) (Ch 20).

1273
01:08:24.329 --> 01:08:25.204
Part 8.

1274
01:08:25.484 --> 01:08:26.828
Interview Q and A.

1275
01:08:28.078 --> 01:08:28.910
Beginner.

1276
01:08:29.190 --> 01:08:30.214
Question 1.

1277
01:08:30.494 --> 01:08:33.929
Explain ACID with an example from an e-commerce order.

1278
01:08:34.209 --> 01:08:35.574
Thirty-second answer:

1279
01:08:35.854 --> 01:08:41.358
Atomicity: order, items, stock reservation and outbox commit together or not at all.

1280
01:08:41.638 --> 01:08:48.934
Consistency: declared constraints (unique order id, available = 0, foreign keys) hold after commit.

1281
01:08:49.214 --> 01:08:55.721
Isolation: concurrent transactions don't see each other's partial work, to the degree of the isolation level.

1282
01:08:56.001 --> 01:09:00.822
Durability: once committed, the order survives a crash (WAL fsync).

1283
01:09:02.172 --> 01:09:03.175
Question 2.

1284
01:09:03.455 --> 01:09:05.695
What is an index, and when does it not help?

1285
01:09:05.975 --> 01:09:07.340
Thirty-second answer:

1286
01:09:07.620 --> 01:09:15.343
A sorted structure (a B+ tree) mapping key values to row locations, so lookups and ranges avoid full scans.

1287
01:09:15.623 --> 01:09:29.447
It doesn't help when the query doesn't use the index's leftmost prefix, when a filter matches a large fraction of rows (a scan is cheaper), when functions are applied to the column without an expression index, or on tiny tables.

1288
01:09:30.797 --> 01:09:31.842
Question 3.

1289
01:09:32.122 --> 01:09:36.218
Normalization versus denormalization: when do you denormalize?

1290
01:09:36.498 --> 01:09:37.863
Thirty-second answer:

1291
01:09:38.143 --> 01:09:41.237
Normalize writes so each fact lives in one place.

1292
01:09:41.517 --> 01:09:51.842
Denormalize deliberately for read performance or for historical truth: snapshotting price, tax and address on an order is correct because they must not change later.

1293
01:09:52.122 --> 01:09:56.431
Read models built from events are denormalized copies with a clear owner.

1294
01:09:57.781 --> 01:09:58.848
Question 4.

1295
01:09:59.128 --> 01:10:01.283
What is a transaction isolation level?

1296
01:10:01.563 --> 01:10:02.928
Thirty-second answer:

1297
01:10:03.208 --> 01:10:12.168
It defines which concurrency anomalies a transaction can observe: dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantoms, lost updates, write skew.

1298
01:10:12.448 --> 01:10:22.603
The levels are READ UNCOMMITTED, READ COMMITTED (the PostgreSQL default), REPEATABLE READ (snapshot isolation in PostgreSQL) and SERIALIZABLE.

1299
01:10:23.953 --> 01:10:24.955
Intermediate.

1300
01:10:25.235 --> 01:10:26.345
Question 5.

1301
01:10:26.625 --> 01:10:30.209
How do you prevent overselling inventory under high concurrency?

1302
01:10:30.489 --> 01:10:31.854
Thirty-second answer:

1303
01:10:32.134 --> 01:10:38.705
Use a single atomic conditional update, UPDATE stock SET available = available -:q WHERE...

1304
01:10:38.985 --> 01:10:42.803
AND available =:q, and treat 0 rows as sold out.

1305
01:10:43.083 --> 01:10:45.195
Add CHECK (available = 0).

1306
01:10:45.475 --> 01:10:52.665
For multi-item orders, do it in one transaction with lines locked in sorted order, and roll back if any line fails.

1307
01:10:54.015 --> 01:10:55.124
Question 6.

1308
01:10:55.404 --> 01:10:59.009
Optimistic versus pessimistic locking: when do you use each?

1309
01:10:59.289 --> 01:11:00.655
Thirty-second answer:

1310
01:11:00.935 --> 01:11:09.532
Optimistic (a version column, 0 rows = conflict) suits low-contention edits and user-facing concurrency (ETags).

1311
01:11:09.812 --> 01:11:11.241
Pessimistic (SELECT...

1312
01:11:11.521 --> 01:11:18.455
FOR UPDATE) suits high-contention or multi-step logic that can't be one statement, at the cost of holding locks.

1313
01:11:18.735 --> 01:11:22.020
For counters, an atomic conditional update beats both.

1314
01:11:23.370 --> 01:11:24.458
Question 7.

1315
01:11:24.738 --> 01:11:28.557
What is the N+1 problem, and how do you fix it in JPA?

1316
01:11:28.837 --> 01:11:30.202
Thirty-second answer:

1317
01:11:30.482 --> 01:11:36.605
Loading N parents, then lazily loading each parent's association, issues 1 + N queries.

1318
01:11:36.885 --> 01:11:53.823
Fix it with fetch joins or @EntityGraph for single queries, batch fetching (@BatchSize or defaultbatchfetchsize) for collections, DTO projections for read APIs, and a two-query pattern (page parents, then fetch children by IN list) when paginating.

1319
01:11:55.173 --> 01:11:56.197
Question 8.

1320
01:11:56.477 --> 01:11:58.717
How do you size a HikariCP pool?

1321
01:11:58.997 --> 01:12:00.363
Thirty-second answer:

1322
01:12:00.643 --> 01:12:07.021
From Little's Law: busy connections = request rate times DB time per request, plus headroom.

1323
01:12:07.301 --> 01:12:16.688
Split across pods, and check that Σ pods times pool fits the database's connection budget, which should stay near a small multiple of DB cores.

1324
01:12:16.968 --> 01:12:20.232
Keep connection-timeout short, and use a fixed-size pool.

1325
01:12:21.582 --> 01:12:22.649
Question 9.

1326
01:12:22.929 --> 01:12:27.430
Explain MVCC and why long-running transactions are dangerous.

1327
01:12:27.710 --> 01:12:29.075
Thirty-second answer:

1328
01:12:29.355 --> 01:12:34.987
MVCC keeps multiple row versions so readers see a snapshot without blocking writers.

1329
01:12:35.267 --> 01:12:39.342
Old versions are removed by vacuum once no snapshot needs them.

1330
01:12:39.622 --> 01:12:50.353
A long-running (or idle-in-transaction) session keeps an old snapshot alive, so vacuum can't clean anything newer, and tables and indexes bloat across the whole database.

1331
01:12:51.703 --> 01:12:52.471
Senior.

1332
01:12:52.751 --> 01:12:53.796
Question 10.

1333
01:12:54.076 --> 01:12:55.889
You're seeing deadlocks during peak.

1334
01:12:56.169 --> 01:12:58.089
How do you diagnose and fix them?

1335
01:12:58.369 --> 01:12:59.735
Thirty-second answer:

1336
01:13:00.015 --> 01:13:06.436
Read the deadlock report in the PostgreSQL logs (the processes, the waiting locks and the statements).

1337
01:13:06.716 --> 01:13:13.692
Typical causes are multi-row updates in inconsistent orders, or foreign-key checks and triggers taking locks.

1338
01:13:13.972 --> 01:13:28.543
Fix by locking in a deterministic order (sort by key), keeping transactions short, avoiding lock upgrades (read then update the same row, which should be a single UPDATE), and adding bounded retries for 40P01.

1339
01:13:29.893 --> 01:13:31.023
Question 11.

1340
01:13:31.303 --> 01:13:33.693
What is write skew, and how do you prevent it?

1341
01:13:33.973 --> 01:13:35.274
Give a real example.

1342
01:13:35.554 --> 01:13:36.919
Thirty-second answer:

1343
01:13:37.199 --> 01:13:43.450
Two transactions each read a condition, then write different rows, and together they violate an invariant.

1344
01:13:43.730 --> 01:13:49.703
For example, each rider checks at least one other rider is on shift at this store and both go off shift.

1345
01:13:49.983 --> 01:13:53.418
REPEATABLE READ (snapshot isolation) doesn't prevent it.

1346
01:13:53.698 --> 01:13:58.434
Prevent it with SERIALIZABLE (and retries), by locking a common row (SELECT...

1347
01:13:58.714 --> 01:14:06.287
FOR UPDATE on the store's shift record), or by materializing the invariant as a constraint or counter updated atomically.

1348
01:14:07.637 --> 01:14:08.725
Question 12.

1349
01:14:09.005 --> 01:14:12.056
Walk me through reading an EXPLAIN plan for a slow query.

1350
01:14:12.336 --> 01:14:13.701
Thirty-second answer:

1351
01:14:13.981 --> 01:14:16.029
Run EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS).

1352
01:14:16.309 --> 01:14:18.656
Find the node with the most actual time.

1353
01:14:18.936 --> 01:14:35.341
Compare estimated versus actual rows (big gaps mean statistics problems), look for Seq Scans with large Rows Removed by Filter (a missing index), nested loops over large outer sets, sorts spilling to disk, and high buffer reads (cache misses).

1354
01:14:36.691 --> 01:14:37.971
Question 13.

1355
01:14:38.251 --> 01:14:42.198
How do you change the schema of a hot, large table without downtime?

1356
01:14:42.478 --> 01:14:43.843
Thirty-second answer:

1357
01:14:44.123 --> 01:14:47.409
Expand, then migrate, then contract across releases.

1358
01:14:47.689 --> 01:14:49.353
Add nullable columns first.

1359
01:14:49.633 --> 01:14:50.913
Backfill in batches.

1360
01:14:51.193 --> 01:14:53.966
Add constraints as NOT VALID and then validate.

1361
01:14:54.246 --> 01:14:55.910
Create indexes CONCURRENTLY.

1362
01:14:56.190 --> 01:15:00.329
Set locktimeout so migrations fail fast instead of queuing traffic.

1363
01:15:00.609 --> 01:15:04.619
Only drop or rename after no running code uses the old shape.

1364
01:15:05.969 --> 01:15:07.164
Question 14.

1365
01:15:07.444 --> 01:15:10.068
When and how do you use read replicas safely?

1366
01:15:10.348 --> 01:15:11.713
Thirty-second answer:

1367
01:15:11.993 --> 01:15:17.476
Route read-only, staleness-tolerant queries to replicas (browsing, history, dashboards).

1368
01:15:17.756 --> 01:15:26.481
Keep decision reads and anything right after a user's write on the primary, or verify the replica has replayed the write's LSN (read-your-writes).

1369
01:15:26.761 --> 01:15:30.217
Monitor lag, and never run analytics on OLTP replicas.

1370
01:15:31.567 --> 01:15:32.463
Architect.

1371
01:15:32.743 --> 01:15:33.938
Question 15.

1372
01:15:34.218 --> 01:15:38.421
The orders database is growing 2 TB a year and writes are rising.

1373
01:15:38.701 --> 01:15:39.682
What's your plan?

1374
01:15:39.962 --> 01:15:41.327
Thirty-second answer:

1375
01:15:41.607 --> 01:15:44.637
First, measure (top queries, write mix, working set).

1376
01:15:44.917 --> 01:16:01.642
Then, in order: fix queries and indexes; partition orders by month (pruning, cheap retention); archive cold partitions to object storage or a warehouse; move reads to replicas and read models; reduce write amplification (fewer indexes, batching).

1377
01:16:01.922 --> 01:16:13.293
Only when a single primary's write capacity is on a runway shorter than the migration lead time, plan sharding (by customer or region) or a distributed SQL database.

1378
01:16:14.643 --> 01:16:15.837
Question 16.

1379
01:16:16.117 --> 01:16:20.747
How do you choose isolation levels and locking strategies as a platform standard?

1380
01:16:21.027 --> 01:16:22.392
Thirty-second answer:

1381
01:16:22.672 --> 01:16:41.403
Default to READ COMMITTED with atomic statements for counters and invariants, optimistic versions for user edits, pessimistic locks with deterministic order where logic can't be one statement, and SERIALIZABLE only for complex cross-row invariants (ledgers) with mandatory retry wrappers.

1382
01:16:41.683 --> 01:16:43.624
Declare invariants as constraints.

1383
01:16:43.904 --> 01:16:48.448
Encode the rules in shared libraries, review checklists and architecture tests.

1384
01:16:49.798 --> 01:16:51.142
Question 17.

1385
01:16:51.422 --> 01:16:54.067
Should each microservice have its own database?

1386
01:16:54.347 --> 01:16:56.011
What about reporting across them?

1387
01:16:56.291 --> 01:16:57.657
Thirty-second answer:

1388
01:16:57.937 --> 01:17:06.726
Yes: database-per-service (or schema-per-service with enforced ownership) keeps services independently deployable and prevents hidden coupling.

1389
01:17:07.006 --> 01:17:15.689
Cross-service reporting goes through events or CDC into a warehouse or lakehouse (Ch 21), not cross-database joins.

1390
01:17:15.969 --> 01:17:18.273
Share data via APIs and events.

1391
01:17:19.623 --> 01:17:20.796
Question 18.

1392
01:17:21.076 --> 01:17:26.409
Managed PostgreSQL versus self-managed on Kubernetes: how do you decide?

1393
01:17:26.689 --> 01:17:28.055
Thirty-second answer:

1394
01:17:28.335 --> 01:17:38.425
Managed services (RDS or Aurora, Cloud SQL) give automated backups, patching, failover and monitoring, which is the right default for most teams.

1395
01:17:38.705 --> 01:17:54.791
Self-managed (operators like CloudNativePG on Kubernetes) gives control, portability and possibly lower cost at scale, but needs real DBA and SRE expertise for backups, upgrades, failover testing and storage performance.

1396
01:17:56.141 --> 01:17:57.378
Question 19.

1397
01:17:57.658 --> 01:18:01.242
How do you design a ledger (wallet) in a relational database?

1398
01:18:01.522 --> 01:18:02.887
Thirty-second answer:

1399
01:18:03.167 --> 01:18:13.685
Use append-only double-entry records: every transfer inserts balanced debit and credit entries in one transaction (the sum is zero), and entries are never updated.

1400
01:18:13.965 --> 01:18:24.013
Balances are either derived (sum of entries) or kept as a cached balance row updated atomically with a CHECK (balance = 0) in the same transaction.

1401
01:18:24.293 --> 01:18:30.927
Use idempotency keys for every movement, and reconcile daily with external sources (Razorpay settlements).

1402
01:18:32.277 --> 01:18:33.387
Question 20.

1403
01:18:33.667 --> 01:18:37.059
When would you move a workload off PostgreSQL entirely?

1404
01:18:37.339 --> 01:18:38.704
Thirty-second answer:

1405
01:18:38.984 --> 01:18:47.859
When the access pattern fits another model much better, and the relational benefits (transactions, joins, constraints) aren't needed for that workload.

1406
01:18:48.139 --> 01:19:06.592
Examples: very high write rates of time-series or events (a time-series or LSM store), full-text relevance search (a search engine), large key-value session state (Redis or DynamoDB), graph traversals, or analytical scans over TBs (a columnar warehouse).

1407
01:19:06.872 --> 01:19:10.456
Keep the system of record relational where correctness matters.

1408
01:19:11.806 --> 01:19:12.702
Part 9.

1409
01:19:12.982 --> 01:19:14.390
Summary and Practice.

1410
01:19:15.640 --> 01:19:16.728
Key takeaways.

1411
01:19:17.008 --> 01:19:24.005
ACID gives you only what you declare and choose: constraints for consistency, isolation levels for concurrency.

1412
01:19:24.285 --> 01:19:28.168
At READ COMMITTED, app-level read-modify-write loses updates.

1413
01:19:28.448 --> 01:19:33.376
Use atomic conditional updates for counters, and back invariants with CHECK constraints.

1414
01:19:33.656 --> 01:19:40.781
MVCC lets readers and writers coexist, but long transactions block vacuum and cause bloat everywhere.

1415
01:19:41.061 --> 01:19:43.216
Indexes are sorted B+ trees.

1416
01:19:43.496 --> 01:19:50.707
Design composite, covering and partial indexes from access patterns, and verify with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS).

1417
01:19:52.057 --> 01:19:59.139
Lock in a deterministic order to prevent deadlocks, keep transactions short, and never make remote calls inside them.

1418
01:19:59.419 --> 01:20:04.006
Write skew needs SERIALIZABLE, a common lock, or a materialized counter.

1419
01:20:04.286 --> 01:20:06.334
Snapshot isolation isn't enough.

1420
01:20:06.614 --> 01:20:08.043
JPA pitfalls:

1421
01:20:08.323 --> 01:20:14.190
N+1, open-in-view, IDENTITY disabling batching, collection fetch + pagination.

1422
01:20:14.470 --> 01:20:16.241
Assert query counts in tests.

1423
01:20:16.521 --> 01:20:21.747
Pool size is set by Little's Law and the database's capacity, not by guesswork.

1424
01:20:22.027 --> 01:20:25.121
Use server-side statement, lock and idle timeouts.

1425
01:20:25.401 --> 01:20:30.158
Replicas need read-your-writes handling (LSN tokens) and lag monitoring.

1426
01:20:31.508 --> 01:20:36.649
Grow with partitioning, archiving and online migrations before considering sharding.

1427
01:20:37.999 --> 01:20:39.322
Decision cheat sheet.

1428
01:20:39.602 --> 01:20:40.967
Here is a quick table:

1429
01:20:41.247 --> 01:20:42.719
Situation, and Choose.

1430
01:20:42.999 --> 01:20:46.050
Counter with an invariant (stock, slots, coupons):

1431
01:20:46.330 --> 01:20:47.183
UPDATE...

1432
01:20:47.463 --> 01:20:49.234
SET x = x - n WHERE...

1433
01:20:49.514 --> 01:20:51.306
AND x = n + CHECK.

1434
01:20:51.586 --> 01:20:52.994
User editing a record:

1435
01:20:53.274 --> 01:20:56.623
Optimistic version or ETag (Ch 04).

1436
01:20:56.903 --> 01:20:59.527
Multi-row logic that can't be one statement:

1437
01:20:59.807 --> 01:21:00.639
SELECT...

1438
01:21:00.919 --> 01:21:03.437
FOR UPDATE in sorted order, short tx.

1439
01:21:03.717 --> 01:21:05.999
Cross-row invariant (write skew risk):

1440
01:21:06.279 --> 01:21:09.842
SERIALIZABLE + retry, or a materialized counter.

1441
01:21:10.122 --> 01:21:11.466
Work queue in the DB:

1442
01:21:11.746 --> 01:21:13.154
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED.

1443
01:21:13.434 --> 01:21:14.821
Filter + sort list:

1444
01:21:15.101 --> 01:21:19.154
Composite index matching WHERE equality + ORDER BY + keyset.

1445
01:21:19.434 --> 01:21:21.333
Only a small subset is queried:

1446
01:21:21.613 --> 01:21:22.743
Partial index.

1447
01:21:24.093 --> 01:21:25.565
Huge time-ordered table:

1448
01:21:25.845 --> 01:21:29.344
Range partitioning by time (+ BRIN for some cases).

1449
01:21:29.624 --> 01:21:31.523
Read-heavy, staleness-tolerant:

1450
01:21:31.803 --> 01:21:34.000
Replica + read-your-writes tokens.

1451
01:21:35.350 --> 01:21:36.481
